.el .ds Aq '
.TH git-veil-reveal 1  "git-veil-reveal " 
.SH NAME
git\-veil\-reveal \- Decrypt all tracked files back to plaintext
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgit\-veil reveal\fR [\fB\-\-email\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-gpg\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-stdin\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Decrypt all tracked files back to plaintext
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-email\fR \fI<EMAIL>\fR
Your email address
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR \fI<REMOTE>\fR [default: origin]
Git remote name
.TP
\fB\-\-gpg\-home\fR \fI<GPG_HOME>\fR
Key store directory (default: $HOME/.git\-veil)
.TP
\fB\-\-passphrase\-stdin\fR
Read the passphrase for a passphrase\-protected private key from stdin (exactly one line). Wins over the GITVEIL_PASSPHRASE environment variable; never pass a passphrase as a CLI argument
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH EXTRA
Decrypts every tracked file whose .secret ciphertext exists back to its
plaintext path, using your private key from the local key store. The
keyring signature is verified against the pinned trusted key before any
decryption. Your identity comes from git config user.email or \-\-email;
passphrase\-protected keys take the passphrase from GITVEIL_PASSPHRASE or
\-\-passphrase\-stdin.

reveal is the inverse of hide for all files; unhide does one file and
deletes its ciphertext; cat prints one file without touching disk state.

EXAMPLES
  $ git\-veil reveal
  $ git\-veil reveal \-\-email alice@example.com
  $ git\-veil reveal \-\-passphrase\-stdin < pass.txt
